 "final": "The spec is set up and passes validation with no findings. The render shows the fixed x = 0 face and the two lug loads on the x = 160 end face, where the brief puts them.\n\n- **Domain:** 160 \u00d7 80 \u00d7 80 mm, steel (E = 210 GPa, \u03bd = 0.3), volume fraction 0.3, 2 mm elements. That gives an 80 \u00d7 40 \u00d7 40 grid of 128,000 elements.\n- **Support:** the whole x = 0 face is fixed in x, y and z (1,681 nodes).\n- **Loads:** each lug is a 16 \u00d7 16 mm patch on the end face, both centred at z = 40.\n  - The top lug (y 64\u201380) carries +625 N in z.\n  - The bottom lug (y 0\u201316) carries \u2212625 N in z.\n  - The lug centres are 64 mm apart, so 625 N \u00d7 64 mm = 40,000 N\u00b7mm = 40 N\u00b7m about +x.\n  - Each patch selects 81 nodes.\n- **Lug blocks:** I added a 4 mm thick block behind each lug (x 156\u2013160) that is always kept solid, so the lugs stay attached to the part.\n\nDONE. Assumptions:\n- The lugs are 16 \u00d7 16 mm areas on the end face, and each one's force is spread evenly over its nodes.\n- The force on each lug is 625 N, worked out from the 64 mm distance between the lug centres.\n- The 4 mm deep solid lug blocks are my addition; the brief doesn't ask for them.\n- The bolted end is modelled as the whole x = 0 face fully fixed.",
